        <div id="real-deal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is Bowflex’s Black Friday Price a Real Deal?</h2>
<p>
            A real Black Friday deal is a price lower than the one the machine has actually been selling for,
            not just lower than a regular price nobody pays. Bowflex’s regular prices fail that test: on every
            date our pages record since early October, all three treadmills were on sale below them. So the
            comparison that matters is against the sale prices we recorded earlier, on dated pages of this
            site.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[620px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Earlier prices we recorded</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Black Friday price (24 Nov)</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Verdict</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T6</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$999 on sale, $1,299 regular (bowflex.com, early October, our T6 review); $899 (bowflex.com, 14 Oct)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$899</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 below early October; matches 14 Oct. A good price, not a new low</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T9</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399 on sale, $1,799 regular (bowflex.com, our T9 review, published 14 Oct); $1,499 on sale (bowflex.com, 20 Oct)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,399</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 below 20 Oct; matches mid-October</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,899 on sale, $2,299 regular (bowflex.com, 14 Oct); $1,999 (bowflex.com, 5 Nov)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999 plus free shipping</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$100 above 14 Oct; about level once delivery is counted</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>What this tells you.</strong> Bowflex’s Black Friday prices are not invented: $899 and
            $1,399 are real prices, and they are the lowest we have recorded for the T6 and T9. But they are
            not new. Bowflex charged exactly the same in mid-October, and it moved the T9 up to $1,499 within a
            week, which shows how often its sale prices change. The T16 is the odd one out: its Black Friday
            price is higher than the $1,899 we saw on 14 October.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why the T16 is closer than it looks.</strong> When we checked on 14 October, Bowflex charged
            $99 for standard delivery to the main entrance, porch, garage or driveway. This week the T16 offer
            includes free shipping. If that free shipping covers standard delivery, the delivered total is
            $1,999 now against $1,998 in October: a dollar apart. If you were going to pay for room-of-choice
            ($249) or white-glove ($349) delivery anyway, check at checkout how much of that the free shipping
            takes off, because Bowflex’s offer line does not say.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What we would treat as a good Bowflex price.</strong> $899 or less for the T6, $1,399 or
            less for the T9, and $1,999 or less delivered for the T16. We have no evidence that Bowflex will
            go lower on Cyber Monday and will not guess at one. If prices change after this week, judge the new
            price against these figures, not against the regular prices. Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday method guide</a> explains how inflated regular
            prices work across the industry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="terms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How Bowflex’s Black Friday Offer Works</h2>
<p>
            Unlike Horizon, which uses a different code for each treadmill this week, Bowflex simply shows the
            sale price on the product page. The small print under each offer on 24 November set out the rules:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Dates:</strong> the T6 and T9 offers expire on 27 November 2026 at 11:59 pm Central
            time; the T16 offer expires a day earlier, on 26 November at 11:59 pm Central, which is
            Thanksgiving.</li>
            <li><strong>Where:</strong> valid only within the 48 contiguous states. Bowflex says it cannot ship
            to P.O. boxes, APO/FPO addresses, US territories, Canada or international destinations.</li>
            <li><strong>One offer:</strong> “not available with any other offer”, valid once per customer, and
            subject to availability. Bowflex adds that the price is subject to change at any time.</li>
            <li><strong>No retroactive discounts:</strong> the offers exclude previously placed orders. If you
            paid more a week ago, the terms do not promise the difference back, though it costs nothing to ask
            within the 30-day return window.</li>
            <li><strong>Online only:</strong> the offers are “not valid at retail stores or outlets”.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>The price match promise.</strong> Bowflex’s Price Match Promise page says that if you find a
            lower price or a better shipping offer on the same Bowflex product from one of its authorised
            retailers, and you contact its sales team before you buy, it will match it on bowflex.com. The
            offer must be sold and shipped by that retailer, in stock and live online. Third-party marketplace
            sellers, including third-party sellers on Amazon and Walmart, are excluded, as are clearance,
            open-box and refurbished prices, membership-only prices, and anything that needs a coupon or
            promotional code. So a lower price from an authorised retailer selling direct could be matched; a
            third-party marketplace listing on Amazon could not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Financing and pre-tax payment.</strong> Bowflex’s home page on 24 November advertised 36
            months at 0% APR through Bread Pay for qualifying customers, for checkouts completed between
            22 November and 5 December 2026 with a $200 minimum; the small print says rates range from 0% to
            34.99% APR depending on credit. The site also offers HSA or FSA payment through Truemed for buyers
            who qualify. Our <a href="/are-treadmills-hsa-fsa-eligible/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">HSA and FSA guide</a> explains who that
            applies to; if you do, the tax saving can be worth more than the Black Friday discount.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-model" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Bowflex to Buy at Black Friday Prices</h2>
<p>
            All three treadmills share Bowflex’s standard warranty, listed on each product page on
            24 November: lifetime on the frame and motor, three years on mechanical parts and one year on
            labour. All three top out at 12 mph and 15% incline, and Bowflex says none needs a subscription.
            The differences are deck width, weight rating and the console.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking and jogging, most buyers: T6 at $899.</strong> Bowflex lists a 20 by 60-inch
            running path, speeds to 12 mph, up to 15% motorized incline, a 325 lb maximum user weight and a
            dual LED/LCD console, with Apple Watch connection and JRNY, Peloton and Zwift on your own device.
            It folds to 44 inches long. At $899 it is the cheapest full-size Bowflex, and the deal to take if a
            20-inch deck suits you. Our <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6 review</a> covers it in
            detail.</li>
            <li><strong>Runners and heavier users: T9 at $1,399.</strong> A 22 by 60-inch running path, a
            350 lb rating, a backlit 7-inch colour LCD and ten built-in programs, per Bowflex. The extra two
            inches of width matter if you run, drift on the belt, or are broad-shouldered. See our <a href="/bowflex-t9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T9 review</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Screen and the highest rating: T16 at $1,999.</strong> The T9’s 22 by 60-inch deck
            and footprint with a 16-inch HD touchscreen that runs JRNY, Netflix, Spotify and a web browser,
            and a 375 lb rating. Buy it if you will watch the screen; if you will prop up a tablet, the T9 does
            the running for $600 less. Our <a href="/bowflex-t16-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T16 review</a> has the full
            specification.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are weighing up a used or discontinued Bowflex instead, our <a href="/bowflex-treadmill-10/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex Treadmill 10</a> page explains what changed between the old
            range and the current three, and why the T9 is its closest replacement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Each Step Up Costs This Week</h2>
<p>
            Because the T16’s saving is $100 smaller than the T6’s and T9’s, the cost of moving to the top
            model is higher this week than at regular prices. Using Bowflex’s prices on 24 November 2026:
          </p>
